<p>A whole program compiles in a few milliseconds into a module of one to a few tens of kilobytes, with no interpreter bundled in: the module is the program, plus the runtime helpers it calls (float formatting, string case mapping, and the like), each built only when a program uses it. Optimization is where the time goes. Binaryen costs 4 to 14 times the rest of the pipeline and takes 6 to 54% off the module, so it is worth running for something you ship and worth skipping in a fast edit loop, which is what <code>CompilerOptions { optimize: false, ..CompilerOptions::default() }</code> is for. The first three rows are end-to-end programs from the repository; <code>nested_collections</code> is the feature example with the largest module, as a second data point at a different shape.</p>

<div class="callout">
    <p><strong>The rule the compiler holds itself to:</strong> a construct either produces Python's answer or fails loudly. It never quietly produces a different one.</p>
    <p>There are two documented exceptions. Integer width is the first: <code>int</code> is a 32-bit two's-complement value, so <code>1000000 * 1000000</code> wraps to <code>-727379968</code> where CPython answers <code>1000000000000</code>. If your arithmetic can exceed 32 bits, your program is not in the supported subset. The second is a NaN inside a collection, which does not equal itself (see the list below). Both are explained under <a href="/docs/numbers/">Numbers</a>.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="shapes">Shapes that do not compile</h2>

<p>Tagged by what happens when you try. <span class="not-prose">{{ tag("refused") }}</span> is a compile error you will see immediately; <span class="not-prose">{{ tag("traps") }}</span> stops the module at run time instead of answering; <span class="not-prose">{{ tag("differs") }}</span> is a case where the answer is not Python's; <span class="not-prose">{{ tag("workaround") }}</span> means there is a supported way to write the same thing.</p>

<div class="not-prose my-6 {{ codecls }}">
<ul class="divide-y divide-line">

{% call shape("Module-level statements other than definitions", ["refused", "workaround"]) %}
A loop, <code>if</code>, <code>try</code>, bare call, augmented assignment, tuple unpacking, or write through a subscript or attribute at module level. A WebAssembly module has no top-level run step, so the statement would be compiled away. <code>X = 1</code>, <code>X = helper()</code>, <code>X = ClassName()</code>, <code>def</code>, <code>class</code>, and imports (a <code>try</code>/<code>except ImportError</code> guarded import included) do work, and a definition that is not a plain constant is evaluated once, at instantiation, in source order, so every function shares the same list or instance. Move the rest into a function, or under <code>if __name__ == "__main__":</code>.
{% endcall %}

{% call shape("<code>async def</code>, <code>await</code>, <code>async for</code>, <code>async with</code>", ["refused"]) %}
There is no event loop in the output.
{% endcall %}

{% call shape("Starred parameters on methods, <code>**</code> unpacking in a call, positional-only parameters", ["refused"]) %}
Functions and <code>__init__</code> take <code>*args: T</code> (a tuple of <code>T</code>), <code>**kwargs: T</code> (a <code>dict[str, T]</code>), and keyword-only parameters, <code>f(*xs)</code> passes a list or tuple on as <code>*args</code>, and keyword arguments work in every call. Other methods take neither starred nor keyword-only parameters, a call cannot unpack a dict with <code>**</code>, and <code>/</code> is refused. Every call to one of the module's own functions is checked against its signature, so a surplus or missing argument is a compile-time <code>TypeError</code>.
{% endcall %}

{% call shape("<code>match</code>, <code>del name</code>, <code>except*</code>, <code>type</code> aliases, <code>from x import *</code>", ["refused"]) %}
Not implemented. Each is reported by name with its location, and so is <code>del</code> of an attribute or a stepped slice. <code>global</code>, <code>nonlocal</code>, nested <code>def</code>, <code>assert</code>, conditional expressions, and <code>del</code> of a list index, list slice, or dict key do work.
{% endcall %}

{% call shape("Metaclass shapes beyond <code>__new__</code>, <code>__init__</code>, and <code>__call__</code>", ["refused"]) %}
Multiple inheritance works, along CPython's C3 method resolution order with cooperative <code>super()</code>, and so do metaclasses: a metaclass's <code>__new__</code> and <code>__init__</code> run once for each class it makes, specialized at compile time, and its <code>__call__</code> replaces instantiation. The class <code>namespace</code> argument, <code>__prepare__</code>, creating a class by calling the metaclass, and class keywords other than <code>metaclass=</code> are refused.
{% endcall %}

{% call shape("An override that changes the signature", ["refused", "workaround"]) %}
Every implementation of an overridden method is reached through one indirect call, which names a single WebAssembly signature, so <code>def m(self)</code> in the base and <code>def m(self, k: int)</code> in the subclass is refused. Give the override the same parameters and return type.
{% endcall %}

{% call shape("Indexing, measuring, or calling a method on a parameter nothing types", ["refused", "workaround"]) %}
A lambda's parameters, and a nested function's unannotated ones, take their types from the calls in their scope when those agree, and a <code>sorted()</code> key's from the elements it is given, so <code>sorted(pairs, key=lambda kv: kv[1])</code> works. A parameter nothing types (a lambda only passed on to an unannotated parameter, or called with an int in one place and a str in another) holds an untyped word, so <code>kv[1]</code> or <code>len(w)</code> on it is refused rather than answering from that word. Use a named <code>def</code>, whose parameters can be annotated.
{% endcall %}

{% call shape("A decorator the compiler cannot apply", ["refused", "workaround"]) %}
The method kinds, <code>@dataclass</code>, <code>@abstractmethod</code>, <code>@functools.total_ordering</code>, <code>@functools.singledispatch</code> with <code>@f.register</code>, the caching decorators (accepted, no effect), and decorators the program writes itself all work on module functions: a user decorator is applied at compile time and specialized for each function it wraps, factories and stacks included. A user decorator on a method or a class, a wrapper that uses <code>*args</code> other than to pass it on, <code>@cached_property</code>, <code>@singledispatchmethod</code>, and a decorator name the program never defines are refused, and so are calls to <code>functools.partial</code>, <code>reduce</code>, and <code>cmp_to_key</code>. Write the wrapper's work inline, or use <code>@property</code>.
{% endcall %}

{% call shape("Comparing collections whose element types differ or are unknown", ["refused", "workaround"]) %}
Tuples and lists compare by value, and a tuple works as a set member or a dict key. Comparing two with different element types (<code>(1, 2) == (1.0, 2)</code>, True in CPython) or with element types the compiler cannot see is refused rather than read at the wrong width, and so are equality between dicts and a list, dict, or set used as a set member or dict key (unhashable in CPython). Annotate the collections (<code>List[Tuple[int, int]]</code>) so both sides are known.
{% endcall %}

{% call shape("Mixing types in one collection", ["refused", "workaround"]) %}
Every slot in a collection is read at one width, so a literal mixing types no one type holds (<code>[1, "a"]</code>, <code>[1, True]</code>) is refused; use a class or a tuple for a record. An int written into a float collection is widened for you (<code>xs.append(3)</code> on a <code>List[float]</code>). The other direction is refused, because storing <code>2.5</code> where the slot is read as an integer would truncate it. Make the elements one type.
{% endcall %}

{% call shape("Reading a field through an unannotated parameter", ["refused", "workaround"]) %}
<code>def total(cart): return cart.count</code> has no type for <code>cart</code>, so the read is refused with a hint rather than answering 0. Annotate the parameter (<code>cart: Cart</code>). The rich comparison methods are the exception: an unannotated <code>other</code> in <code>__eq__</code>, <code>__lt__</code>, and the rest is typed as the class.
{% endcall %}

{% call shape("A string, float, collection, or instance through an untyped place", ["refused", "workaround"]) %}
An unannotated parameter or return, and the elements of a bare <code>list</code>, <code>set</code>, or <code>dict</code> parameter or return, hold one word read back as an int. Ints, bools, <code>None</code>, and functions pass through; anything else is refused, since a string would lose its length and a float its width. Annotate the parameter, or write <code>List[str]</code> and <code>Dict[str, int]</code>. A bare annotation on a variable is fine: its elements take the type of the value assigned.
{% endcall %}

{% call shape("<code>x is None</code> on an untyped value, and <code>dict.get(key)</code> without a default", ["refused", "workaround"]) %}
<code>None</code> is the word 0, which an untyped value can also be, so the test cannot be answered there. An <code>Optional[int]</code>, <code>Optional[bool]</code>, or <code>Optional[float]</code> is boxed, so <code>is None</code>, <code>==</code>, and truthiness on one give CPython's answer; arithmetic between two optional numbers is refused. <code>dict.get(key)</code> with no default is refused where the values are ints, floats, or strings, which have no distinct word for the <code>None</code> a missing key answers; pass a default.
{% endcall %}

{% call shape("<code>str.format</code> or <code>%</code> on a format string built at run time", ["refused"]) %}
The whole format specification mini-language works in f-strings, <code>format()</code>, <code>.format()</code>, and <code>%</code>, digit for digit with CPython. <code>.format()</code> and <code>%</code> need a literal format string, and a <code>*</code> width or precision in <code>%</code>, or a precision on <code>%d</code>, <code>%x</code>, or <code>%o</code>, is refused. A spec CPython rejects is a compile error carrying CPython's message when it is a literal, and traps when it is built at run time.
{% endcall %}

{% call shape("<code>**</code> with a fractional or negative exponent", ["traps"]) %}
Powers are computed by repeated multiplication. <code>2.0 ** 0.5</code> traps, because it needs exp/log this runtime does not carry, and a negative integer exponent traps because Python's answer is a float an int cannot hold. <code>float()</code> and <code>int()</code> of a string with non-ASCII digits or spaces trap too, where CPython would accept them.
{% endcall %}

{% call shape("Two modules defining the same function name", ["refused", "workaround"]) %}
Merged modules share one namespace, so <code>utils.format</code> next to <code>report.format</code> is a compile error naming both files. Rename one.
{% endcall %}

{% call shape("Object lifetime", ["differs"]) %}
The allocator has no <code>free</code>: every instance lives until the module is torn down, and <code>__del__</code> is never invoked. A long-running program that allocates in a loop will grow its memory, and a generator abandoned mid-iteration does not run its <code>finally</code> blocks until <code>close()</code> is called.
{% endcall %}

{% call shape("A NaN inside a collection", ["differs"]) %}
With <code>x = float("nan")</code>, <code>(x,) == (x,)</code> and <code>[x] == [x]</code> are False here, where CPython answers True: floats are unboxed values, so there is no object identity for CPython's same-object shortcut to find. <code>x == x</code> is False in both.
{% endcall %}

{% call shape("Tracebacks", ["differs"]) %}
No traceback is kept. An exception a <code>__main__</code> block does not catch writes only the last line of CPython's report (<code>ValueError: boom</code>) to standard error and exits with status 1.
{% endcall %}

{% call shape("Third-party packages, and most of the standard library", ["refused"]) %}
No PyPI. Standard-library support is small so far: the module constants (<code>math.pi</code>, <code>os.sep</code>, ...), <code>sys.stdin</code>, <code>sys.stdout</code>, <code>sys.stderr</code>, <code>sys.argv</code>, and <code>sys.exit()</code>, <code>re.sub</code> and <code>re.escape</code> on constant arguments, and the <code>functools</code> decorators. Every other function in <code>sys</code>, <code>os</code>, <code>os.path</code>, <code>math</code>, <code>random</code>, <code>json</code>, <code>re</code>, <code>datetime</code>, <code>logging</code>, <code>collections</code>, <code>itertools</code>, and <code>functools</code> is refused at compile time, and each is tracked on the <a class="link" href="/modules/">modules board</a>.
{% endcall %}

</ul>
</div>

